	annote = {East Palestine Response Timeline Summaries
East Palestine Train Derailment Response: Four Phase Summary
Phase 1: February 3 – February 20, 2023
Immediate Emergency Response and Vent and Burn
The Derailment (February 3, 2023) At approximately 8:54 p.m. on February 3, 2023, Norfolk Southern Railway train 32N derailed 38 railcars at milepost 49.5 in East Palestine, Ohio (NTSB, p. 11). The derailment occurred after a wheel bearing overheated on hopper car GPLX75465. Three tank cars carrying hazardous materials were mechanically breached during the derailment and released flammable materials, which ignited a fire that grew to involve 35 railcars (NTSB, p. 11).
Emergency Response (February 3–4) Emergency responders from 48 agencies across Ohio and Pennsylvania responded to the incident (NTSB, p. 24). The East Palestine Fire Department deputy chief established a command post about 400 feet from the derailment site and initially ordered firefighters to spray water on the burning railcars (NTSB, p. 24). However, the tactic proved insufficient, and the fire grew larger than responders had ever seen. At approximately 11:00 p.m., the incident commander ordered a 1-mile evacuation affecting approximately 2,000 residents (NTSB, p. 27). The evacuation was ordered through the Wireless Emergency Notification System (WENS) and the federal Integrated Public Alert \& Warning System (NTSB, p. 27).
Response was hampered by several challenges:


Delayed consist information: East Palestine dispatch requested train consist information from Norfolk Southern at 9:04 p.m. on February 3, but Norfolk Southern did not return the call. The first emailed consist reached responders at approximately 10:00 p.m. (NTSB, p. 25)


Radio interoperability issues: Emergency responders lacked common radio channels, forcing them to rely on emails, phone calls, and paper copies to share information (NTSB, p. 88)


Fire-damaged placards: Placards identifying hazardous materials on several tank cars became illegible after heat exposure, making it difficult to identify which materials were involved (NTSB, p. 93)


Vent and Burn Decision and Execution (February 4–6) Five tank cars containing vinyl chloride monomer (VCM) were not mechanically breached in the derailment but were exposed to fires. Between February 4 and 5, these tank cars released material through pressure relief devices (PRDs) (NTSB, p. 12). On February 4, Norfolk Southern contractors observed an energetic PRD actuation on tank car OCPX80179, which they interpreted as evidence of polymerization—a potentially dangerous chemical reaction (NTSB, p. 32).
After discussions with contractors, the incident commander agreed to a vent and burn procedure, which involves deliberately breaching tank cars with explosives to release and ignite their contents. At 4:37 p.m. on February 6, contractors detonated charges on all five VCM tank cars, releasing and igniting their contents (NTSB, p. 15). The incident commander later testified that he felt "overwhelmed and blindsided" by the decision and was given only 13 minutes to decide before the procedure needed to begin (NTSB, p. 43).
Evacuation Lifted and Rail Service Resumed (February 8–9) At 5:28 p.m. on February 8, the evacuation order was lifted after meetings with the governors of Ohio and Pennsylvania, the Environmental Protection Agency, and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (NTSB, p. 46). Norfolk Southern resumed rail service through East Palestine on main track 2 on February 8, and main track 1 reopened on February 9 (NTSB, p. 46).
Health and Environmental Monitoring Begins The Unified Command—comprising U.S. EPA, Ohio EPA, Columbiana County Emergency Management Agency, the Village of East Palestine, and Norfolk Southern—implemented a "phased multilayered approach" to measure air quality, including sorbent tubes, summa canisters, the EPA's TAGA bus, passive badges, and handheld devices (“East Palestine, Ohio Train Derailment Unified Command”, p. 1). The Ohio Department of Health opened a clinic in East Palestine on February 19, 2023 (“Ohio Department of Health to open clinic in East Palestine {\textbar} Ohio Department of Health”).

Phase 2: February 21 – October 30, 2023
EPA Order, Dioxin Sampling, and Cleanup Operations
EPA Issues Unilateral Administrative Order (February 21, 2023) On February 21, 2023, the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency issued a Unilateral Administrative Order requiring Norfolk Southern to conduct and pay for all cleanup actions associated with the derailment (US EPA, 2023). This order formalized EPA's authority to oversee the cleanup and ensured Norfolk Southern would bear all costs.
Dioxin Sampling and Analysis In late February 2023, EPA directed Norfolk Southern to sample for dioxins that may have been associated with soot deposition from the February 6 vent and burn of vinyl chloride (Devito et al., p. 4). On March 7, 2023, EPA approved Norfolk Southern's sampling plan, which included 8 categories of sites: Residential, Commercial, Agricultural, and Other for both Ohio and Pennsylvania (Devito et al., p. 4).
The sampling plan was designed to assess background dioxin concentrations and compare them to potentially affected areas. Twenty-five sites in the East Palestine area were designated as representative of background soil, with samples collected at two depths (0-1 inch and 1-6 inches) to identify contamination from the vent and burn versus historic deposition (Devito et al., p. 3). The analysis found that for most background samples, many dioxins were below detection limits, with a mean TEQ concentration of 6.7 pg/g soil (Devito et al., p. 3).
Results of the broader sampling showed that only 6 outlier sites out of 146 sampled (approximately 4\%) exceeded screening levels, and these were located in public rights-of-way or on commercial/industrial properties—not on private residential properties (Devito et al., p. 4). The EPA memo concluded that "the vent and burn did not significantly contribute to background dioxin deposition in the area" (Devito et al., p. 9) .
Ongoing Cleanup and Contamination Removal Throughout this period, Norfolk Southern and EPA contractors conducted ongoing cleanup of contaminated soil, water, and debris. The cleanup involved removing affected materials from the derailment site and monitoring air and water quality in the surrounding community.
NTSB Investigation The National Transportation Safety Board conducted its investigation throughout 2023, including an Investigative Hearing on June 22–23, 2023 (NTSB, p. 1). The NTSB gathered testimony from Norfolk Southern personnel, emergency responders, contractors, Oxy Vinyls representatives, and other stakeholders to understand the derailment cause, emergency response decisions, and the vent and burn procedure.
Health Assessments and Community Support The Assessment of Chemical Exposure (ACE) survey, which collected health symptom information from residents, closed on March 31, 2023 (“East Palestine, Ohio Train Derailment Unified Command”, p. 1). The Ohio clinic established in February continued operating to support residents with health concerns. Health officials encouraged residents experiencing symptoms to contact healthcare providers or the Poison Control Center hotline (1-877-603-0170) (“East Palestine, Ohio Train Derailment Unified Command”, p. 1). Mental health resources were also made available through SAMHSA's Disaster Distress Helpline, Ohio's CareLine, and Pennsylvania's Support \& Referral Helpline (“East Palestine, Ohio Train Derailment Unified Command”, p. 2).

Phase 3: October 31, 2023 – June 27, 2024
Continued Cleanup, Monitoring, and Settlement Development
Long-Term Monitoring Programs Throughout late 2023 and early 2024, environmental monitoring continued under EPA supervision. This included:


Groundwater and creek water sampling and monitoring


Testing of private drinking water wells


Air quality monitoring in the community


These monitoring programs were designed to track any long-term contamination effects from the derailment and vent and burn.
EPA Newsletters and Community Communication EPA continued issuing regular newsletters to keep the East Palestine community informed about response activities. Newsletters were published on multiple dates during this period, including November 1 and November 15, 2023, December 13, 2023, January 10, 2024, February 14, 2024, April 4, 2024, and May 23, 2024 (“Newsletter: East Palestine Train Derailment Response, 11-1-2023 {\textbar} US EPA”)(“Newsletter: East Palestine Train Derailment Response, 11-15-2023 {\textbar} US EPA”)(“Newsletter: East Palestine Train Derailment Response, 12-13-2023 {\textbar} US EPA”)(“Newsletter: East Palestine Train Derailment Response, 1-10-2024 {\textbar} US EPA”)(“Newsletter: East Palestine Train Derailment Response, 2-14-2024 {\textbar} US EPA”)(“Newsletter: East Palestine Train Derailment Response, 4-4-2024 {\textbar} US EPA”)(“Newsletter: East Palestine Train Derailment Response, 5-23-2024 {\textbar} US EPA”).
Federal and State Actions On September 26, 2023, the Federal Register published "Ensuring the People of East Palestine Are Protected Now and in the Future," documenting ongoing federal efforts to support the community (“Ensuring the People of East Palestine Are Protected Now and in the Future”, 2023).
The Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection announced plans in late 2023 to test drinking water near the East Palestine derailment site for ten years (“Shapiro Administration Continues to Advocate for Western Pennsylvania Residents, Announces Plans to Test Drinking Water Near East Palestine Train Derailment Site for Ten Years”).
NTSB Final Report Development The NTSB continued developing its final investigative report during this period. The report would ultimately identify safety issues including:


Failure of systems to identify failing wheel bearings


Inadequate training of volunteer first responders


Delayed transmittal of train consist information


Illegibility of fire-damaged placards


Use of tank cars with documented poor derailment performance


Flawed communication and decision-making leading to the vent and burn


The report was nearing completion during this phase, with release anticipated in 2024.
Settlement Discussions Behind the scenes, the U.S. Department of Justice and EPA were negotiating a settlement with Norfolk Southern regarding the company's liability for environmental damage from the derailment. These discussions would ultimately lead to the proposed consent decree announced in mid-2024.

Phase 4: June 27, 2024 – February 3, 2025
NTSB Report Release and Settlement Agreement
NTSB Final Report Released (June 25, 2024) On June 25, 2024, the NTSB released its final Railroad Investigation Report (RIR-24-05) (NTSB, p. 1). The report determined that:


Probable Cause: The failure of the L1 bearing on the 23rd railcar that overheated and caused the axle to separate, leading to the derailment and post-derailment fire


Contributing factors:


Continued use of DOT-111 tank cars in hazardous materials service


Failure of Norfolk Southern and contractors to communicate relevant expertise and dissenting opinions to the incident commander


Inaccurate representation that tank cars were at risk of catastrophic failure from polymerization


Norfolk Southern's delay in transmitting train consist information


Ohio's insufficient training requirements for volunteer firefighters (NTSB, p. 13)




The NTSB issued 34 new recommendations to multiple entities including the Federal Railroad Administration, Pipeline and Hazardous Materials Safety Administration, state of Ohio, firefighter associations, Norfolk Southern, and chemical industry groups (NTSB, p. 13).
U.S. Department of Justice Settlement with Norfolk Southern On June 27, 2024, the Department of Justice announced a proposed settlement with Norfolk Southern (“United States and Norfolk Southern Railroad Settlement Agreement for East Palestine Train Derailment”, p. 1). The settlement required Norfolk Southern to:
Environmental Cleanup (\$900+ million)


Complete the cleanup (expected fall 2024)


Pay back all money spent by EPA, ensuring no taxpayer funds were used


Continue groundwater and creek water monitoring for 10 years


Take action if contamination is found


Environmental Improvements (\$6 million)


Fund projects to improve and protect Sulphur Run and Leslie Run


Address older pre-derailment pollution


Protect waterways from stormwater runoff


Restore fish populations


Preserve nearby wetlands


Community Health Fund (\$25 million)


Medical monitoring for qualified residents for up to 20 years


Free annual exams including physicals, blood panels, pulmonary function tests, and x-rays


Automatic eligibility for residents within 2 miles of the derailment site and first responders on site in February 2023


Free mental health counseling for residents of Columbiana County (Ohio) and Beaver/Lawrence Counties (Pennsylvania)


Civil Penalty (\$15 million)


Penalty for Clean Water Act violations based on days pollutants spilled into Sulphur Run and the amount of oil released


Railroad Safety Improvements


Install hot bearing detectors every 15 miles on tracks


Lower temperature thresholds for alarms


Improve communication with government and public before vent and burn procedures


Follow new rules for organizing railcars in long trains


Apply high-hazard flammable train rules to more trains, including those carrying flammable gases


Phase out DOT-111 tank cars for flammable hazardous materials


Increase emergency response training and fund state/local participation in annual exercises (“United States and Norfolk Southern Railroad Settlement Agreement for East Palestine Train Derailment”, p. 4)


Settlement Public Comment Period The Department of Justice accepted public comments on the proposed settlement through August 2, 2024 (“United States and Norfolk Southern Railroad Settlement Agreement for East Palestine Train Derailment”, p. 1). Representatives from DOJ were available at EPA's Welcome Center in East Palestine on July 15 and 16, 2024, to answer questions (“United States and Norfolk Southern Railroad Settlement Agreement for East Palestine Train Derailment”, p. 1).
Continued Health and Environmental Programs Throughout this period, EPA newsletters continued updating the community on response activities, with newsletters issued on July 8, 2024, October 31, 2024, and February 18, 2025 (“Newsletter: East Palestine Train Derailment Response, 7-8-2024 {\textbar} US EPA”)(“Newsletter: East Palestine Train Derailment Response, 10-31-2024 {\textbar} US EPA”)(“Newsletter: East Palestine Train Derailment Response, 2-18-2025 {\textbar} US EPA”).
One-Year Anniversary and Ongoing Concerns By the one-year anniversary in February 2024, and continuing through this period, some residents continued to report health concerns potentially linked to the derailment. Researchers began tracking long-term health effects (“Two years after a train derailment and chemical disaster in East Palestine, some residents worry about liver cancer”). Trains with toxic chemicals continued to roll through East Palestine, with few legislative steps taken to prevent similar disasters (Murphy et al., 2025).

Summary of Key Response Actions by Phase




Phase


Key Actions




Phase 1 (Feb 3–20, 2023)


Derailment; emergency response; 1-mile evacuation; vent and burn (Feb 6); evacuation lifted (Feb 8); rail service restored; initial air monitoring; health clinic opened (Feb 19)




Phase 2 (Feb 21–Oct 30, 2023)


EPA Unilateral Administrative Order (Feb 21); dioxin sampling approved (Mar 7); dioxin results analysis; continued cleanup; NTSB Investigative Hearing (June 22–23); ACE survey closes (Mar 31)




Phase 3 (Oct 31, 2023–Jun 27, 2024)


Continued monitoring and cleanup; EPA newsletters; PA 10-year water testing plan; settlement negotiations; NTSB report finalization




Phase 4 (Jun 27, 2024–Feb 3, 2025)


NTSB report release (June 25); DOJ settlement announcement (June 27); public comment period; settlement finalization; continued health monitoring programs; ongoing environmental monitoring




Created by Beaver · Open Message
},
