Unified Command Composition and Evolution: East Palestine Train Derailment Response
Overview
The East Palestine response transitioned from an initial emergency Incident Command structure to a Unified Command, and eventually to an EPA-led oversight model as the response moved from emergency phase to long-term cleanup and remediation.

Phase 1: February 3 – February 20, 2023
Incident Command → Unified Command Formation
Initial Incident Command (February 3–4, 2023)
The response began with the East Palestine Fire Department (EPFD) serving as the Incident Commander during the immediate emergency phase:

Deputy Fire Chief, EPFD: Served as initial incident commander from approximately 9:00 p.m. on February 3, establishing the first command post about 400 feet from the derailment pileup (NTSB, p. 24)
Fire Chief, EPFD: Arrived at approximately 2:00 a.m. on February 4 and assumed incident commander role (NTSB, p. 28)

Transition to Unified Command
By February 20, 2023, the response had formalized into a Unified Command structure comprising five core member organizations:

U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)
Columbiana County Emergency Management Agency (CCEMA)
Village of East Palestine
Ohio Environmental Protection Agency (Ohio EPA)
Norfolk Southern Corporation (“East Palestine, Ohio Train Derailment Unified Command”, p. 2)

Supporting/Cooperating Agencies
The Unified Command received assistance from:

Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection
Pennsylvania Department of Health (“East Palestine, Ohio Train Derailment Unified Command”, p. 2)

Key Contractors Supporting Response

Specialized Professional Services, Inc. (SPSI): Emergency response contractor certified by The Chlorine Institute; began arriving on scene around 11:00 p.m. on February 3 (NTSB, p. 27)
Specialized Response Solutions (SRS): Another Chlorine Institute-certified contractor; began supporting response on February 5 (NTSB, p. 35)
CTEH: Environmental consulting contractor providing modeling services (NTSB, p. 36)
Explosive Service International (ESI): Contractor conducting vent and burn procedure (NTSB, p. 40)

Federal Agencies Supporting Response (Not Unified Command Members)

Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA): Deployed 70 personnel to coordinate multiagency effort (US EPA, p. 2)
National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB): Leading independent investigation (US EPA, p. 2)
Department of Transportation (DOT): Pipeline and Hazardous Materials Safety Administration (PHMSA) and Federal Railroad Administration (FRA) staff on scene (US EPA, p. 2)

Command Post Relocations

Initial post near derailment site ({\textasciitilde}400 feet) – February 3
EPFD/EPPD shared station – February 4 (by 2:00 a.m.)
Public school ({\textgreater}1 mile from site) – Morning of February 5 (NTSB, pp. 27-28)


Phase 2: February 21 – October 30, 2023
Unified Command Operations Period
Unified Command Members (Continuing)
The five core organizations remained in Unified Command through this period:

U.S. EPA
CCEMA
Village of East Palestine
Ohio EPA
Norfolk Southern Corporation

EPA's Increased Authority (February 21, 2023)
On February 21, 2023, EPA issued a Unilateral Administrative Order requiring Norfolk Southern to conduct and pay for all cleanup actions (US EPA, 2023). This strengthened EPA's oversight role within the Unified Command structure.
FEMA Integration (February 17, 2023 onward)
FEMA announced on February 17, 2023 that it would deploy an assistance team to "support ongoing operations, including incident coordination and ongoing assessments of potential long-term recovery needs" (“FEMA reverses course, will send assistance team to East Palestine following train derailment”, 2023)
Federal Interagency Teams (February 24, 2023 onward)
Federal interagency teams began door-to-door canvassing of East Palestine homes on February 24, 2023 (US EPA, 2023)
Documented Unified Command Activity
As of March 20, 2023, the EPA update document explicitly stated: "See operational updates from other agencies supporting this incident response below" followed by sections on FEMA, DOT/NTSB, and Norfolk Southern, indicating these agencies continued coordinated operations (US EPA, p. 2)
By April 14, 2023, updates noted: "Norfolk Southern and the Unified Command will continue to work closely as soil removal and remediation work gets underway" (“Updates from East Palestine Response - April 14, 2023”, p. 1)
Key Personnel Changes

Incident Commander: The EPFD Fire Chief continued serving as incident commander through at least the vent and burn decision on February 6 (NTSB, p. 43)
After the initial emergency phase (post-February 9 when rail service resumed), incident command likely transitioned to EPA as the lead agency for environmental remediation


Phase 3: October 31, 2023 – June 27, 2024
Transition to EPA-Led Oversight
Evolution Away from Unified Command Structure
By late 2023, the response had transitioned from the emergency phase (where Unified Command structure is typical) to a more traditional EPA-led oversight model. Notable changes include:

October 19, 2023 update: No mention of "Unified Command"; instead states "EPA ordered Norfolk Southern to conduct additional cleanup" (“Updates from East Palestine Response - October 19, 2023”, p. 1)
September 8, 2023 update: References "Ohio EPA" making determinations about wastewater (“Updates from East Palestine Response - September 8, 2023”, p. 1); no mention of Unified Command
March 29, 2024 update: States "Norfolk Southern began sediment cleanup work in Sulphur Run, in accordance with the workplan approved by EPA on March 1" (“Updates from East Palestine Response - March 29, 2024”, p. 1); again, no Unified Command reference

Lead Agency: U.S. Environmental Protection Agency
EPA maintained oversight and approval authority:

Issued orders under Clean Water Act authority (“Updates from East Palestine Response - October 19, 2023”, p. 1)
Approved workplans before cleanup activities (“Updates from East Palestine Response - March 29, 2024”, p. 1)
Conducted confirmatory sampling and oversight (“Updates from East Palestine Response - October 19, 2023”, p. 1)
Maintained Welcome Center for community engagement (“Updates from East Palestine Response - March 29, 2024”, p. 1)

Responsible Party: Norfolk Southern Corporation

Continued executing cleanup under EPA oversight
Conducted soil removal, wastewater treatment and shipment
Implemented approved workplans for creek cleanup

Supporting State Agencies

Ohio EPA: Continued making technical determinations (e.g., wastewater characterization) (“Updates from East Palestine Response - September 8, 2023”, p. 1)
Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection: Continued coordination for Pennsylvania-impacted areas

Federal Presence

NTSB: Continued investigation; held Investigative Hearing June 22–23, 2023; released final report June 25, 2024 (NTSB, p. 1)
FEMA: Transitioned from operational support to recovery coordination


Phase 4: June 27, 2024 – February 3, 2025
Settlement Implementation Period
Formal Structure: EPA Oversight with Norfolk Southern as Responsible Party
By this phase, the response had fully transitioned to a regulatory oversight framework governed by:

Lead Federal Agency: U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (Region 5)
Responsible Party: Norfolk Southern Corporation (under EPA orders and settlement)
State Partners: Ohio EPA, Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection

Department of Justice Settlement (June 27, 2024)
The June 27, 2024 settlement formalized the ongoing structure:

Consent Decree: Documented EPA's oversight authority and Norfolk Southern's obligations
Public Comment Period: August 2, 2024 deadline for community input (“United States and Norfolk Southern Railroad Settlement Agreement for East Palestine Train Derailment”, p. 1)
Community Coordination: Representatives from DOJ available at EPA Welcome Center July 15–16, 2024 (“United States and Norfolk Southern Railroad Settlement Agreement for East Palestine Train Derailment”, p. 1)

Ongoing Responsibilities

Norfolk Southern: Continued cleanup completion; 10-year groundwater/creek monitoring; Community Health Fund administration (“United States and Norfolk Southern Railroad Settlement Agreement for East Palestine Train Derailment”, pp. 2-3)
EPA: Oversight of cleanup completion; approval of monitoring programs; public communication (newsletters continued through February 18, 2025) (“Newsletter: East Palestine Train Derailment Response, 2-18-2025 {\textbar} US EPA”)

Long-Term Monitoring Entities

Community Health Program: Includes Coordination Committee of community members and Independent Advisor (“United States and Norfolk Southern Railroad Settlement Agreement for East Palestine Train Derailment”, p. 3)
Pennsylvania: Maintained 10-year water testing commitment announced in 2023 (“Shapiro Administration Continues to Advocate for Western Pennsylvania Residents, Announces Plans to Test Drinking Water Near East Palestine Train Derailment Site for Ten Years”)


Summary Table: Command Structure Evolution



































Time PeriodCommand StructureLead AgencyKey PartnersFeb 3–9, 2023Incident Command → Unified CommandEPFD (initially), then EPA-led UCU.S. EPA, Ohio EPA, CCEMA, Village of East Palestine, Norfolk Southern, PA DEP, PA DOHFeb 10–Oct 30, 2023Unified CommandEPA (UC lead)Same five core UC members; FEMA, DOT, NTSB in supporting rolesOct 31, 2023–Jun 27, 2024EPA-led OversightU.S. EPA (Region 5)Norfolk Southern (responsible party), Ohio EPA, PA DEPJun 27, 2024–Feb 3, 2025Settlement-Governed OversightU.S. EPA (under Consent Decree)Norfolk Southern (bound by settlement), state agencies

Key Observations


Unified Command Duration: The formal Unified Command structure was operational from approximately February 5/6, 2023 through at least April 2023 (explicitly mentioned in April 14, 2023 update), and likely continued in some form through October 2023, though references become less frequent by late 2023.


Incident Commander Transition: The EPFD Fire Chief served as incident commander during the critical initial emergency phase (including the vent and burn decision on February 6), but incident command transitioned to EPA as the response shifted from emergency to environmental remediation.


EPA's Evolving Role: EPA progressed from Unified Command member → lead agency within Unified Command → sole lead agency with oversight authority → settlement-enforcing authority.


Norfolk Southern's Consistent Role: Throughout all phases, Norfolk Southern remained the "responsible party"—the entity legally obligated to conduct and pay for response actions, though under shifting oversight frameworks (UC, EPA orders, consent decree).

